# .gitignore — Orion's Belt seed (G10, post-v1.0.0 adversarial review).
# Covers only what the harness ITSELF generates at runtime and should never be
# committed — it does not try to be a generic language/stack .gitignore (that is
# project content). First install: created from scratch. If the project already
# has a .gitignore, Copier treats it like any other pre-existing file (conflict
# prompt / manual merge) — same mechanics as any template file.

# Visual evidence (PNG + manifest per run) — regenerated on every capture, not a
# committable source of truth (ui-evidence skill, manual chapter 09).
{{ harness_evidence_dir }}/

# Durable state of long runs (marathon) — RUN.md per in-progress run; a work
# artifact, not history to preserve (chapter 08).
{{ harness_runs_dir }}/

# Original-request ledger (request-ledger.py) — verbatim user prompts per session,
# re-injected on SessionStart to fight objective drift. Local-only working state,
# and may hold sensitive prompt text; never committed.
.harness/requests/

# Proof-of-completion manifests (proof_evidence.py). The `prova-de-conclusao`
# skill mandates `.harness/evidence/<run>.json` — a DIFFERENT directory from
# `{{ harness_evidence_dir }}/` above, which is the ui-evidence PNG dir. Only the
# latter was covered, so every verdict the completion gate demands produced an
# untracked file that `git add -A` would commit. The manifest binds a claim to
# HEAD + a worktree fingerprint: stale by construction on the next commit,
# and worthless as history.
.harness/evidence/

# PID registry read by dev-doctor/reap-leaks (HARNESS_PID_REGISTRY_DIR — hardcoded
# to .harness/pids in harness.conf.jinja, not a Copier question). Machine-local
# PIDs and start tokens: meaningless on any other machine, and actively harmful
# if a stale entry travels into someone else's checkout.
.harness/pids/

# Installer state (install_apply.py: BACKUP_ROOT_REL / JOURNAL_REL). The backup
# tree holds VERBATIM COPIES of the target's pre-merge files — committing it
# duplicates project content and can publish whatever a merged file contained.
# The journal is transaction state for rollback; it survives a killed install
# and is consumed by the next run.
.harness/install-backups/
.harness/install-journal.json

# Python bytecode of the modules shipped under .harness/lib/ — the hooks import
# _tooling_conf.py via `python3 .../script.py` on every fire (Edit/Write/Stop),
# which materializes __pycache__/ on the very first commit if this .gitignore is
# not in place before `git add -A`.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
